'use strict';
const {course,section:s}=require('./web-helper');
const w=(code,expect,problem,steps,explanation)=>({lang:'javascript',code,expect,problem,steps,explanation});
const units=[
{id:'js-values-coercion',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Trace values, coercion and strict equality',introduction:'An online shop receives quantities as form strings while its pricing code expects numbers. JavaScript can convert values implicitly, so a result that looks plausible may be string concatenation rather than arithmetic.',sections:[s('Distinguish a value from its representation','JavaScript has primitive values including strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, bigint and symbols, plus objects. A quoted "2" is a string, not the number 2. The + operator can concatenate strings after its conversion steps, while other arithmetic operators can request numeric conversion. Do not assume every operator treats a string the same way.','Strict equality === avoids the type coercion performed by ==, but still has specific semantics: NaN is not equal to itself, +0 and -0 compare equal, and objects compare by identity. Object.is has different treatment of NaN and signed zero; it does not perform a deep comparison of objects.'),s('Convert at a deliberate boundary','Parse and validate external values against the actual contract. Number("") is zero, so conversion success alone does not establish that a required field was supplied. Number.isFinite on a converted number can help reject infinities and NaN, but allowed ranges and integer rules still need explicit checks.','The worked code executes actual JavaScript in isolated Node. It demonstrates the selected language expressions, not a browser form pipeline or server-side authorization. Currency precision, localization and domain-specific rounding need separate decisions rather than assuming binary floating-point is exact for every price.')],worked:w('console.log("2" + 3);\nconsole.log(Number("2") + 3);\nconsole.log("2" === 2);\nconsole.log(Number.isNaN(Number("not-a-number")));','23\n5\nfalse\ntrue','Trace a quantity string through concatenation, explicit conversion and strict comparison.',['Evaluate the string-plus-number expression.','Convert the intended numeric input explicitly.','Compare types strictly and inspect a failed conversion.'],'The outputs are actual ECMAScript expression results under the isolated runtime. They do not validate a complete purchase request.'),mistakes:['Treating a form string as a trusted numeric quantity.','Using === as if it performed deep object comparison.'],takeaways:['Trace operand types before predicting an operator.','Convert and validate at explicit input boundaries.'],quiz:[
['What is the value type of "7"?','string','number','boolean','undefined','Quotation marks make this literal a string rather than numeric data.','Numeric-looking text is still text.','It is not a true/false literal.','A supplied string is not missing.'],
['What does "2" + 3 produce?','The string "23"','The number 5','The number 23','A guaranteed TypeError','The plus operator performs string concatenation for this operand combination.','Explicit conversion would be needed for this arithmetic intent.','The result is a string, not a number.','This expression is valid JavaScript.'],
['What does strict equality avoid?','Equality type coercion','Every numeric edge case','Object identity semantics','Every need for validation','Strict equality does not convert unlike operand types to make them equal.','NaN and signed-zero rules still exist.','Objects still compare by identity.','Business input still needs validation.'],
['A blank required quantity becomes Number("") and gives 0. What should validation check first?','Input presence','Only whether conversion ran','Only the field’s CSS class','Only the button’s label','Explicit presence rules prevent empty input from being mistaken for a valid quantity.','Conversion can succeed with unintended meaning.','Styling does not validate data.','Labels do not enforce presence.'],
['Two separate objects have the same fields. What does === compare?','Their object identity','Every nested property recursively','Their JSON text automatically','Only their key count','Strict equality on objects checks whether both operands reference the same object.','No deep comparison occurs.','Serialization is not implicit equality.','Equal key counts do not define identity.'],
['A shop needs a positive integer quantity after conversion. What is still required?','Domain range/integer checks','Only string concatenation','Only a successful page render','Only an arbitrary default of one','A finite converted number can still be fractional, zero or negative and violate the contract.','Concatenation does not enforce quantity rules.','Rendering is not input validation.','An arbitrary default can hide invalid input.'],
['Which operation can detect a numeric NaN without coercing a string?','Number.isNaN','Only loose equality to NaN','Only value === NaN','Only Boolean(value)','Number.isNaN checks the numeric NaN value without the coercion of global isNaN.','NaN is not loosely equal to itself.','NaN is not strictly equal to itself.','Truthiness does not specifically identify NaN.'],
['An isolated example prints the expected quantity conversion. What is not proven?','The full business operation is safe','The expression was executed','The supplied conversion returned a number','The displayed result was observed','A language example does not test every validation, authorization, storage or financial rule.','Execution is the actual evidence.','That local expression is checked.','The output is recorded.']
]},
{id:'js-bindings-scope',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Use let and const with block scope',introduction:'A registration workflow has a selected course, a mutable count and several temporary loop values. Clear binding rules reduce accidental reassignment and prevent temporary names from leaking into unrelated parts of the program.',sections:[s('A binding and its referenced object differ','Let creates a reassignable block-scoped binding. Const creates a block-scoped binding that must be initialized and cannot later be reassigned. Const does not freeze an object: properties of the referenced object can still change unless another mechanism constrains them. Use a binding policy that communicates whether the reference is expected to change.','A block is delimited by braces in statements such as if and for. A block-scoped name is available according to its lexical scope, and accessing let/const before initialization in that scope triggers the temporal dead zone. Var has different function/global scoping and initialization behavior, so do not transfer let rules blindly to older code.'),s('Read nested names from the nearest scope','An inner block can declare its own name that shadows an outer binding. Updating the inner binding then does not necessarily change the outer one. This can be intentional, but excessive shadowing makes debugging harder. A loop declared with let can create per-iteration bindings used by closures.','The fixture executes actual nested blocks and a const object mutation. It does not claim that all mutation is desirable; immutable data conventions and deep-freeze behavior are distinct design decisions.')],worked:w('let count = 1;\n{ let count = 3; console.log(count); }\nconsole.log(count);\nconst selection = {course: "web"};\nselection.course = "javascript";\nconsole.log(selection.course);','3\n1\njavascript','Trace a shadowed block binding and a mutable property behind a const reference.',['Read the inner count within its block.','Read the separate outer count after the block.','Modify a property without reassigning the const binding.'],'The results distinguish binding reassignment from object mutation. Const is not deep immutability.'),mistakes:['Assuming const recursively freezes referenced objects.','Reading a block-scoped variable before its initialization.'],takeaways:['Use lexical scope to explain which binding a name references.','Keep reference stability and object mutation separate.'],quiz:[
['What does const prevent for its binding?','Reassignment','All object property changes','Every nested array mutation','Every function call','Const prevents rebinding the name but does not recursively freeze the referenced value.','Properties may remain mutable.','Nested arrays may remain mutable.','Functions can still be invoked.'],
['What scope do let declarations normally have?','Block scope','Only database scope','Only request-header scope','Universal cross-file scope','Let is scoped lexically to the containing block and related declaration context.','Databases are unrelated to lexical declarations.','HTTP headers are not lexical scopes.','Names do not automatically become universal.'],
['What can accessing let before initialization cause?','A ReferenceError','An automatic value of zero','A guaranteed empty string','A successful account login','The temporal dead zone disallows access before initialization in that scope.','No numeric default is supplied by this rule.','No string default is guaranteed.','Binding access is not authentication.'],
['An inner block declares the same count name. Which binding does an inner read normally use?','The nearest lexical binding','Always the outermost binding','Always a property on window','Always the first Git commit','Lexical resolution uses the nearest applicable scope.','Shadowing can select the inner name.','Block locals need not be global object properties.','Repository history does not resolve names.'],
['A const settings object changes its theme property. Is that alone a const violation?','No, the binding stayed the same','Yes, every property is frozen','Only if the theme is a string','Only on mobile browsers','Mutation of the referenced object differs from reassigning the const name.','Const does not freeze properties.','The value type does not change the rule.','Device class does not define const semantics.'],
['A team wants no reassignment but permits a cart array to grow. Which declaration can fit?','const for the array reference','Only a forbidden global variable','Only a CSS custom property','Only a quoted JSON key','Const can preserve the reference while array methods mutate the object under the team’s policy.','A global is not required.','CSS variables are unrelated.','A JSON key is not a JavaScript binding declaration.'],
['Why can repeated shadowing reduce maintainability?','It obscures which binding is read','It automatically encrypts data','It removes every closure','It guarantees a faster loop','Humans must track multiple names with the same spelling across scopes.','No encryption is implied.','Closures can still exist.','Performance is not guaranteed by shadowing.'],
['The scope fixture prints 3 then 1. What explains it?','Two distinct lexical bindings','A server overwrote the variable','The number changed type twice','The browser loaded another stylesheet','The inner and outer count declarations are separate bindings.','No server is involved.','Both values are numbers.','Stylesheets do not select lexical bindings.']
]},
{id:'js-truthy-defaults',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Choose truthiness and defaults without losing valid data',introduction:'A dashboard displays zero completed tasks, an empty optional note and a missing server field. These states should not be accidentally collapsed into the same fallback just because they all look “empty” to a casual reader.',sections:[s('Boolean conversion has defined values','JavaScript conditionals convert values to boolean when needed. False, zero, negative zero, empty string, null, undefined, NaN and zero bigint are falsy; ordinary empty arrays and objects are truthy. The browser-specific legacy document.all behavior is not a general reason to invent other exceptions.','The logical OR operator returns an operand, not necessarily a boolean. It selects the right operand when the left is falsy. Nullish coalescing ?? uses the right operand only for null or undefined, preserving valid zero, false and empty strings. Choose the operator according to the domain’s meaning of absence.'),s('Guard property access with a clear contract','Optional chaining ?. short-circuits on null or undefined at the relevant boundary. It does not validate a returned value, turn a nonfunction into a callable function or rescue an undeclared root identifier. A result of undefined may still require an explicit loading, missing-data or error state.','The fixture compares zero through OR and nullish fallback, then observes truthiness and a missing optional field. The outputs are language semantics, not a complete UI-state specification. Decide separately whether an empty string is valid user data.')],worked:w('const completed = 0;\nconsole.log(completed || 10);\nconsole.log(completed ?? 10);\nconsole.log(Boolean([]));\nconsole.log(({profile: null}).profile?.name ?? "Not supplied");','10\n0\ntrue\nNot supplied','Preserve a valid zero while handling a genuinely missing nested profile.',['Compare OR with nullish coalescing for zero.','Inspect the truthiness of an empty array.','Read an optional nested value and provide a deliberate fallback.'],'The expressions test supplied values only. Whether zero or an empty string is valid remains a business-contract decision.'),mistakes:['Using || when zero is meaningful data.','Assuming empty arrays are falsy.'],takeaways:['Distinguish falsy from missing.','Use optional access and defaults as contract choices, not validation substitutes.'],quiz:[
['Which ordinary value is truthy?','An empty array','The number zero','The empty string','undefined','An ordinary array is an object and is truthy even when it contains no elements.','Zero is falsy.','The empty string is falsy.','Undefined is falsy.'],
['Which values trigger the right side of ?? ?','null and undefined','Every falsy value','Every empty array','Every object without keys','Nullish coalescing distinguishes missing nullish values from other falsy values.','Zero, false and empty strings are preserved.','Arrays are not nullish.','Objects are not nullish merely because empty.'],
['What does || return in JavaScript?','One of its operands','Always a boolean','Always a number','Always the left operand','Logical OR selects and returns an operand according to truthiness.','It does not automatically convert its returned value to boolean.','Operand values can have many types.','Falsy left operands select the right side.'],
['A task count of zero becomes “unknown” through count || "unknown". What fits better if zero is valid?','count ?? "unknown"','Always add one to count','Convert every count to true','Delete the field from the response','Nullish fallback preserves zero while handling null/undefined absence.','Adding one corrupts the meaning.','Boolean conversion loses the numeric value.','Deletion hides valid evidence.'],
['A profile is null and profile?.name is read. What is the expression result?','undefined','A guaranteed empty string','A new profile object','The string "null.name"','Optional chaining short-circuits at a nullish value and yields undefined.','No empty-string default is implicit.','No object is created.','The access is not converted into that string.'],
['A field is present but contains an invalid negative age. Does ?. validate it?','No, validation remains separate','Yes, every optional chain validates','Only if ?? follows it','Only if the field name is short','Optional chaining handles nullish access, not domain constraints on returned values.','There is no general validation.','A fallback does not enforce age rules.','Name length is irrelevant.'],
['A function field exists but is a number. Does optional call syntax make it callable?','No, type still matters','Yes, numbers become functions','Yes, calls always return null','Only inside an array','Optional calling short-circuits nullish absence but does not make a noncallable value callable.','No automatic function conversion occurs.','A nonfunction can still cause a TypeError.','Array membership does not define callability.'],
['A UI treats empty notes as intentionally blank. Which fallback choice needs care?','An OR fallback','An explicit contract','A nullish presence distinction','A test with an empty string','OR treats empty string as falsy and may replace an intentional blank.','An explicit contract helps.','Nullish distinctions can preserve it.','Testing that case is appropriate.']
]},
{id:'js-functions-closures',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Trace function calls and retained lexical state',introduction:'A ticketing interface creates separate counters for two queues. A closure lets a function retain access to the lexical environment in which it was created, without making all counters share one global variable.',sections:[s('Functions receive arguments and return values','A function can take parameters, perform work and return a result. Return ends that invocation; without an explicit returned expression, the function returns undefined. Passing an object gives the function a value referencing that object, so property mutation can be visible to the caller even though reassigning the local parameter is different.','A closure combines a function with access to its lexical environment. The environment is not necessarily a frozen snapshot of primitive values: later changes to a captured binding can be observed. Each call that creates a fresh environment can produce independent state.'),s('Follow creation before invocation','In the factory below, makeCounter runs twice and creates two separate count bindings. Repeated calls to one returned function update its own binding. The second counter starts from its own initial state rather than inheriting the first counter’s total.','Closures are useful for encapsulation, callbacks and configuration, but retaining references can also keep data alive. Do not capture large private objects unnecessarily or mistake a closure for a security boundary against all scripts in the same execution environment.')],worked:w('function makeCounter() {\n  let count = 0;\n  return function increment() { count += 1; return count; };\n}\nconst a = makeCounter();\nconst b = makeCounter();\nconsole.log(a(), a(), b());','1 2 1','Trace two independent closure environments.',['Call the factory to create counter a.','Call it again to create a separate counter b.','Invoke both and follow their distinct retained bindings.'],'The example executes language-level closure behavior. It does not test persistence, cross-process coordination or a complete privacy boundary.'),mistakes:['Treating captured bindings as always frozen values.','Using one global counter for logically independent workflows.'],takeaways:['Track function creation and invocation separately.','A closure retains access to its lexical environment.'],quiz:[
['What does a function without an explicit return value return?','undefined','Always zero','Always an empty object','The function’s source text','An invocation that reaches its end without returning an expression yields undefined.','No numeric default is imposed.','No object is created automatically.','Source text is not the return default.'],
['What does a closure retain access to?','Its lexical environment','Every user’s browser history','Every server database automatically','Only copied strings always','A closure can reference bindings from the lexical scope where it was created.','History is not automatically accessible.','Database access requires separate capabilities.','Captured state is not limited to immutable string copies.'],
['Do two factory calls necessarily share one local count binding?','No, fresh calls can be independent','Yes, every local is global','Only if both names are short','Only when the function is an arrow','Each invocation can establish a fresh environment for its returned closure.','Locals are not automatically global.','Names do not define environment sharing.','Arrows are not required for this behavior.'],
['A function mutates a passed object’s status. Why can the caller observe it?','Both references reach that object','The parameter is a global alias always','Every argument is serialized','JavaScript forbids local bindings','The argument value can reference the same object seen by the caller.','It need not be global.','No serialization is implicit.','Local bindings are supported.'],
['A parameter is reassigned to a new object inside a function. What should not be assumed?','The caller’s binding is rebound','The local parameter changed','A new object can exist','The old object can remain referenced','Reassigning the local parameter does not automatically reassign the caller’s variable.','That is the local operation.','Objects can be created.','Other references can retain the original.'],
['A captured variable changes after the closure is created. What can the closure see?','The updated binding value','Only the original text always','Only undefined in every case','Only the variable’s name string','Closures can observe current values of captured bindings rather than immutable snapshots.','Snapshots are not the general rule.','The binding can hold a meaningful value.','Names are not automatically returned as strings.'],
['A callback captures a huge unused customer object. What is a concern?','Unnecessary retained data','Guaranteed faster execution','Automatic secret encryption','Guaranteed garbage collection immediately','Retained references can keep data alive and broaden the amount of state held unnecessarily.','Capturing more data does not guarantee speed.','A closure is not encryption.','Reachable data need not be collected.'],
['The counter example prints 1 2 1. What does the last 1 show?','The second environment is separate','All counters share count=2','The first counter reset globally','The server restored a backup','Counter b starts with its own count binding from its own factory call.','The output contradicts shared count at two.','No global reset occurs.','No server or backup is involved.']
]},
{id:'js-this-calls',comp:'m2.c2',title:'Resolve this from the call style',introduction:'A customer object has a method that formats its display label. Passing that method as a standalone callback can change this, so the same function body may no longer read the intended object.',sections:[s('Ordinary functions have call-dependent this','For an ordinary function, this depends on how it is invoked, not simply where it is written. Calling account.label() ordinarily supplies account as the receiver. Taking const label = account.label and calling label() loses that property-call receiver. Strict-mode plain calls use undefined for this; sloppy-mode behavior differs, so state the execution mode.','Call and apply invoke a function with an explicit receiver and different argument-list forms. Bind creates a function with a fixed receiver and optional leading arguments for later calls. None of these mechanisms performs account authorization; this is a language-level receiver value.'),s('Do not transfer ordinary-function rules to arrows','Arrow functions capture this lexically rather than creating their own call-dependent this. Calling an arrow with call or bind does not replace that captured receiver. This is useful for callbacks that should retain an enclosing receiver but can be wrong for object methods intended to use the call-site object.','The worked example uses strict mode and explicit call/bind, making the receiver assumptions visible. Real event APIs may deliberately supply their own receiver or event target conventions; consult that API rather than infer behavior from all callbacks being functions.')],worked:w('"use strict";\nfunction label(prefix) { return prefix + this.name; }\nconst customer = {name: "Demo"};\nconsole.log(label.call(customer, "Hello "));\nconst fixed = label.bind(customer, "Member ");\nconsole.log(fixed());','Hello Demo\nMember Demo','Supply an ordinary function’s receiver explicitly, immediately and for later use.',['Invoke with call using the customer receiver.','Create a bound function with a stored leading argument.','Invoke the bound function and observe its retained receiver.'],'The output tests strict language call behavior, not permissions or a browser event’s API-specific receiver convention.'),mistakes:['Assuming this always refers to the object where a function was defined.','Using arrows as dynamic-receiver methods without checking intent.'],takeaways:['Read the actual call expression.','Separate ordinary call-dependent this from lexical arrow this.'],quiz:[
['For an ordinary function, what mainly determines this?','Its invocation style','Only its filename','Only its lexical line number','Only its return type','Ordinary function receiver behavior depends on call form and execution mode.','Files do not automatically choose the receiver.','Definition position is not enough.','Return type does not determine this.'],
['What does bind create?','A bound function for later use','An immediate database transaction','A new DOM element','A guaranteed deep object copy','Bind returns a function with a bound receiver and optional initial arguments.','It is not database binding.','It does not create DOM nodes.','It does not clone all receiver data.'],
['How does arrow this differ?','It is lexically captured','It is chosen by every call','It always points to window','It is always the first argument','Arrows capture this from their enclosing lexical context.','Call-site rebinding does not replace it.','Enclosing context may not be window.','Arguments do not define lexical this.'],
['A method is extracted and called as a plain function in strict mode. What may be lost?','The original receiver','The function body source','Every closure binding','The ability to return','Property-call receiver information is not preserved by merely copying the function value.','The same function body exists.','Lexical closures are a different mechanism.','Functions can still return.'],
['A function needs a known receiver for one immediate invocation. Which tool can fit?','call','Only JSON.stringify','Only Object.keys','Only parseInt','Call invokes an ordinary function with an explicitly supplied this value.','Serialization does not invoke with a chosen receiver.','Key enumeration is unrelated.','Numeric parsing is unrelated.'],
['An arrow is passed to call with a different object. What happens to its captured this?','It remains lexical','It becomes the new object','It is encrypted','It always becomes null','Call cannot override the lexical this binding of an arrow function.','That rule applies to suitable ordinary functions instead.','No encryption occurs.','Null is not an automatic result.'],
['A team uses this.role as a client permission check. What remains required?','Server authorization','Only bind on every callback','Only shorter property names','Only a hidden button','A JavaScript receiver value is not authoritative permission evidence for a server operation.','Binding cannot enforce the server.','Names do not establish trust.','Presentation is not authorization.'],
['The bound label prints Member Demo. What was demonstrated?','A stored receiver and argument','A deep-frozen customer record','A verified account identity','A persisted membership change','Bind supplies a receiver and partial arguments for later function calls.','No freeze occurs.','No authentication is performed.','No storage update occurs.']
]},
{id:'js-arrows-return',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Read concise arrows, parameters and return boundaries',introduction:'A logistics dashboard transforms rows into compact summaries. Arrow syntax can make the transformation readable, but braces and parentheses change whether the body is an expression or a statement block.',sections:[s('Recognize the function body form','An arrow expression body returns its expression implicitly: x => x * 2. A block body such as x => { return x * 2; } needs an explicit return for that result. To return an object literal concisely, wrap it in parentheses, for example x => ({id: x.id}); otherwise braces begin a block and the meaning differs.','Default parameters apply when an argument is undefined, not for every falsy input. Rest parameters gather remaining arguments into an array. Keep functions focused and explain whether they return a new value or mutate an existing object; concise syntax does not itself make a transformation pure.'),s('Trace evaluation before optimizing appearance','A map callback receives a value plus index and array arguments. Passing a function that interprets later arguments differently can cause surprises; do not assume every callback accepts only one argument. Prefer a clear adapter when signatures differ.','The fixture constructs new summary objects with a concise return and shows an explicit block return. It executes JavaScript syntax and output directly. Browser rendering, data authorization and performance at production scale are not inferred from the small array.')],worked:w('const rows = [{id: 1, count: 2}, {id: 2, count: 4}];\nconst summaries = rows.map(row => ({id: row.id, doubled: row.count * 2}));\nconst total = summaries.reduce((sum, row) => { return sum + row.doubled; }, 0);\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(summaries));\nconsole.log(total);','[{"id":1,"doubled":4},{"id":2,"doubled":8}]\n12','Return object literals from concise arrows and aggregate their derived values.',['Wrap the concise object literal in parentheses.','Read the explicit return inside the reduce block.','Compare the constructed array and total.'],'The example tests the declared small transformation and syntax, not every callback signature or a rendering pipeline.'),mistakes:['Forgetting return inside a block arrow body.','Passing a callback with incompatible extra-argument semantics.'],takeaways:['Identify expression bodies versus block bodies.','Make callback inputs and return values explicit.'],quiz:[
['Which arrow implicitly returns a doubled number?','x => x * 2','x => { x * 2; }','x => { }','x => { let y = x * 2; }','An expression body returns the expression without an explicit return keyword.','The block discards that expression without return.','The empty block returns undefined.','The local declaration has no returned value.'],
['How can a concise arrow return an object literal?','Parenthesize the object','Always omit every brace','Use a CSS selector','Make the object a comment','Parentheses disambiguate an object expression from an arrow’s statement block.','Objects still need their literal syntax.','Selectors are unrelated.','Comments do not produce values.'],
['When does a default parameter apply?','For an undefined argument','For every zero argument','For every false argument','For every empty string','Defaults are used for undefined rather than all falsy values.','Zero is preserved unless code handles it otherwise.','False is preserved.','Empty strings do not automatically trigger defaults.'],
['A callback block calculates a value but has no return. What does map receive?','undefined for that invocation','The last expression automatically','A guaranteed empty object','The original item automatically','A block body needs explicit return to provide the calculated value.','That is expression-body behavior, not this block.','No object is invented.','The original item is not an automatic return.'],
['A callback receives item, index and array. What should an adapter respect?','The callee’s argument contract','Only the function’s name length','Only the page title','Only a CSS declaration','Extra callback arguments can change behavior when the supplied function interprets them differently.','Names do not define compatible signatures.','Titles do not decide arguments.','CSS is not a function-call contract.'],
['An arrow mutates row.status. Is it automatically pure because it is concise?','No, syntax is not purity','Yes, every arrow is pure','Only when it uses const','Only when map is called','Mutation is a behavior choice independent of arrow syntax.','Arrows can mutate objects.','Const does not freeze referenced objects.','Map callbacks can perform side effects.'],
['Rest parameters collect remaining arguments into what?','An array','A DNS record','A CSS rule list','A promise automatically','Rest parameter syntax creates an array of remaining arguments.','Network records are unrelated.','CSS lists are a different concept.','Rest collection does not automatically create a promise.'],
['The summaries example outputs total 12. What was aggregated?','The doubled count values','The original id strings','The object key names','The number of CSS classes','The reduce callback adds each summary’s doubled field starting from zero.','IDs are not the summed field.','Keys are not the operands.','There are no CSS classes in the fixture.']
]},
{id:'js-array-transformations',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Choose map, filter and destructuring for data views',introduction:'A support queue contains open and closed tickets, but a compact list should show only open ticket identifiers and summaries. Transformation methods should communicate whether they select items, replace values or aggregate a result.',sections:[s('Keep selection and transformation distinct','Filter creates a new array containing elements whose callback returns a truthy value. Map creates a new array of callback return values. Neither automatically deep-clones the objects inside; returned or retained references can still point to the same objects. Destructuring extracts fields into bindings but does not validate that the response has the correct schema.','Avoid using map only for side effects when its returned array is discarded; forEach or an explicit loop may communicate that intent better. Transformation ordering can affect both meaning and cost: filter before expensive mapping can avoid work for excluded rows, but do not reorder operations when their semantics differ.'),s('Handle missing data deliberately','Destructuring defaults apply to undefined values, not null. If a remote object may be absent, choose an explicit access/validation policy rather than destructuring blindly and suppressing every error. A presentation view should include only fields the user may receive; client filtering cannot repair a server that disclosed private fields.','The worked array contains synthetic tickets and returns a minimized view. It demonstrates actual transformation semantics, not server authorization, network response validation or a complete privacy guarantee.')],worked:w('const tickets = [{id: 1, state: "open", title: "Login"}, {id: 2, state: "closed", title: "Billing"}];\nconst visible = tickets.filter(({state}) => state === "open").map(({id, title}) => ({id, title}));\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(visible));\nconsole.log(tickets.length);','[{"id":1,"title":"Login"}]\n2','Select open tickets and construct a small display projection.',['Use filter for selection.','Destructure only fields needed by the view.','Use map to construct summary objects and confirm the source array length.'],'The result covers synthetic local data. The API must independently prevent unauthorized record and field disclosure.'),mistakes:['Assuming map or filter deeply clones retained objects.','Treating client-side filtering as a server data-access control.'],takeaways:['Choose methods that match selection and transformation intent.','Treat destructuring as access syntax, not schema validation.'],quiz:[
['Which array method selects elements by a predicate?','filter','Only map','Only join','Only push','Filter retains elements whose callback returns a truthy result.','Map transforms return values rather than selecting by itself.','Join produces a string.','Push mutates by appending.'],
['Which method creates one return value per visited element?','map','Only pop','Only includes','Only sort','Map constructs a new array from callback results for the visited elements.','Pop removes an element.','Includes produces a membership boolean.','Sort orders an array in place.'],
['Does destructuring validate an API schema?','No, it extracts values','Yes, every field type is checked','Yes, permissions are checked','Only when const is used','Destructuring accesses fields without supplying a general validation or authorization engine.','Types need explicit checks or appropriate tooling.','Permissions remain separate.','Const does not validate response shapes.'],
['A filtered array contains the same ticket objects. What is true?','References may still be shared','Every ticket was deeply copied','The original objects are frozen','The server records are deleted','A new array does not imply new deep copies of its referenced objects.','Deep copying is not automatic.','No freeze is implied.','Local filtering does not delete server records.'],
['A client removes private fields after receiving them. What remains a problem?','The server already disclosed them','The browser has repaired all privacy','Map encrypts the removed fields','CSS prevents inspecting responses','Clients can inspect delivered data, so the server must minimize authorized responses.','Postdelivery filtering cannot undo disclosure.','Map does not encrypt.','Styling does not hide network payloads securely.'],
['A developer uses map solely to send logs and discards its array. What may communicate intent better?','forEach or a clear loop','Another unused map result','Only JSON parsing','Only a hidden DOM field','A side-effect-oriented iteration can be clearer when no transformed array is needed.','More discarded transformations add noise.','Parsing is a different operation.','DOM fields do not choose iteration semantics.'],
['A destructuring default encounters null rather than undefined. What should be expected?','The default is not automatic','Every null becomes the default','A deep copy always occurs','The value is always a number','Destructuring defaults apply to undefined; null is an explicit value.','Null has different semantics.','Defaults do not deep-copy objects.','No numeric conversion is implied.'],
['The ticket fixture leaves source length at two. What can be concluded?','Those operations kept array length','No objects could ever be shared','Every server request is authorized','Every mutation is impossible','Filter/map here produce a view without changing source length, but shared references and other operations remain possible.','References can still be shared.','No server request is tested.','Other code can mutate data.']
]},
{id:'js-reference-shallow-copy',comp:'m3.c2',title:'Predict aliases and shallow object copies',introduction:'An order editor creates a draft before saving. If the draft shares nested objects with the original, changing a nested shipping value can unexpectedly change the displayed original even though a top-level spread created a new object.',sections:[s('Assignment copies a value, not an arbitrary object graph','Assigning an object reference to another binding makes both bindings refer to that object. A top-level spread such as {...order} creates a new ordinary object populated with the source’s own enumerable properties, but nested object values remain references. Array spread similarly creates a new array with the original element values.','Object.assign and spread have their own details and are not interchangeable for every getter/setter or prototype use case. Neither is a universal deep clone. JSON serialization also has type and structure limitations, such as loss of undefined properties and inability to serialize every value or cycle.'),s('Copy according to the intended update boundary','For an immutable-style update, create new objects along the path being changed and retain untouched references deliberately. Structured cloning supports a defined set of values where available, but still has limits and does not decide what data should be copied for the business operation. A copy does not grant permission to edit the underlying server record.','The example contrasts a shallow aliasing change with a nested copied path. It executes real object-reference behavior in Node and uses synthetic data; it does not measure a complete UI framework’s state propagation.')],worked:w('const original = {id: 7, address: {city: "A"}};\nconst shallow = {...original};\nshallow.address.city = "B";\nconsole.log(original.address.city);\nconst revised = {...original, address: {...original.address, city: "C"}};\nconsole.log(original.address.city, revised.address.city);\nconsole.log(original === shallow);','B\nB C\nfalse','Observe nested aliasing after a shallow copy and a deliberate copied update path.',['Create a new top-level object with spread.','Mutate its shared nested address and inspect the original.','Copy the nested address for an independent revision.'],'The output checks these objects only. It does not make spread a universal deep-clone or an authorization mechanism.'),mistakes:['Calling spread a deep copy.','Using JSON round-tripping as a universal clone without checking data types.'],takeaways:['Track references at every nested level.','Copy the update path intentionally and document sharing.'],quiz:[
['What does assigning an object value to another variable normally copy?','The object reference value','Every nested object recursively','Only a printed JSON string','A server backup','Both bindings can then reach the same object.','Assignment is not automatic deep cloning.','Serialization is not implicit assignment.','No server backup occurs.'],
['What kind of copy does object spread usually create?','A shallow property copy','A universal deep clone','A database transaction copy','An automatic class-instance clone','Spread copies relevant own enumerable properties into a new object while nested references can remain shared.','Nested graphs are not recursively copied.','Transactions are unrelated.','Prototype/class behavior is not universally reconstructed.'],
['Can a new top-level object still share a nested address?','Yes','No, new means all nested values new','Only when address is a string','Only in browser scripts','Nested object values can remain references to the original nested object.','New outer identity does not imply new inner identities.','A string is a primitive, not this nested object case.','This is language behavior in Node too.'],
['A shallow draft changes original.address.city. What explains it?','The nested address is shared','The spread reused outer identity','The server copied the draft back','The string is a global variable','The spread created a new outer object but retained the nested address reference.','Outer identities can differ.','No server operation occurs.','The property is not a global binding.'],
['An update should replace only a nested address city without mutating the old address. What can fit?','Copy the objects along that path','Only copy the variable’s name','Only rename the field in CSS','Only stringify the output label','A new outer object and a new nested address can isolate the intended update path.','Names do not change references.','CSS does not copy data.','Display serialization does not create the needed state structure.'],
['Why is JSON round-tripping not a universal clone?','Some values and graphs differ','It always preserves functions','It always preserves cycles','It automatically preserves every prototype','JSON has defined supported representations and loses or rejects unsupported structures.','Functions are not preserved as executable values.','Cycles normally fail without special handling.','Prototype semantics are not universally retained.'],
['A copied record is editable locally. What remains required to save it?','Server validation and permission','Only its new object identity','Only spread syntax','Only a nonempty title','Local copying does not establish authority or validity for a server mutation.','Identity is not permission evidence.','Syntax is not authorization.','A title does not establish all rules.'],
['The fixture prints original === shallow as false. What should not be inferred?','All nested data is independent','The outer objects differ','Strict identity was compared','A shallow spread was used','Different outer identities do not imply independent nested references.','That is the checked identity result.','The expression is strict equality.','The code explicitly uses spread.']
]},
{id:'js-reduce-aggregation',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Aggregate data with explicit accumulator rules',introduction:'A logistics report totals package counts by depot. Reduce can express a fold over rows, but the accumulator’s initial value and mutation policy must be clear or edge cases become surprising.',sections:[s('Define the accumulator and empty-input behavior','Reduce calls a callback with the accumulator and current item, returning the next accumulator. An explicit initial value makes the empty-input result and expected accumulator type clear. Without an initial value, an empty array throws, and the first item becomes the initial accumulator for nonempty input.','Choose an accumulator that matches the result: a number for a total, a map-like object for grouped values, or another structure. Mutating a fresh local accumulator can be acceptable, but do not accidentally mutate input records or shared external state when the function is intended to be a pure transformation.'),s('Read destructuring and grouping carefully','Destructuring the current row can make the needed fields explicit. Guard group keys according to the actual data contract; plain objects have prototype-related considerations, so a Map or a null-prototype object can be preferable for unconstrained external keys. Do not mix missing, zero and invalid counts without deliberate validation.','The fixture uses a Map with known synthetic depot names and validated numeric counts. It tests grouping and the empty-array initial total, not a complete ingestion schema or the performance of a large reporting pipeline.')],worked:w('const rows = [{depot: "North", count: 2}, {depot: "North", count: 3}, {depot: "South", count: 1}];\nconst grouped = rows.reduce((acc, {depot, count}) => { acc.set(depot, (acc.get(depot) ?? 0) + count); return acc; }, new Map());\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify([...grouped]));\nconsole.log([].reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0));','[["North",5],["South",1]]\n0','Group synthetic counts and define an empty-input total.',['Start from a fresh Map accumulator.','Update the correct depot using an explicit missing-key default.','Read the separate empty-array reduction with initial zero.'],'The output assumes the supplied rows meet their declared contract. It is not validation of arbitrary remote records.'),mistakes:['Omitting the initial accumulator without considering empty input.','Mutating shared source state unintentionally inside a reducer.'],takeaways:['Specify accumulator type and initial value.','Keep grouping keys, numeric validation and mutation policy explicit.'],quiz:[
['What does a reducer callback return?','The next accumulator','Only a boolean predicate','Only the original array','Only a CSS property','Reduce uses each callback’s returned value as the next accumulator.','That describes filtering intent.','The accumulator can have other types.','CSS is unrelated.'],
['What does an explicit initial value clarify?','Type and empty-input behavior','Every server permission','Every nested clone rule','The browser’s viewport width','An initial accumulator defines a starting result even when there are no rows.','Permissions remain separate.','It does not deep-copy all inputs.','Viewport is unrelated.'],
['What happens to [].reduce(callback) without an initial value?','It throws a TypeError','It always returns zero','It always returns an empty Map','It calls the callback once with null','There is no first element to use as an implicit accumulator, so the call fails.','Zero is not an automatic default.','No Map is invented.','No such callback call is supplied by the rule.'],
['A total starts with the string "0" and uses +. What risk should be checked?','String concatenation','Automatic integer validation','Guaranteed numeric zero','Automatic currency rounding','The accumulator’s type affects the plus operation and can produce concatenated strings.','Reduce does not validate integers automatically.','A string is not numeric zero.','No financial rounding policy is supplied.'],
['A grouped report uses untrusted names as object keys. What can be considered?','A Map or safe key policy','Only a bigger title','Only a shorter loop variable','Only a CSS class','Key handling should avoid unintended object/prototype behavior and follow the input contract.','Titles do not constrain keys.','Names do not make keys safe.','CSS does not select data structures.'],
['A reducer mutates a fresh local Map. Is every reducer required to be mutation-free?','No, follow the stated contract','Yes, JavaScript forbids set','Only when counts are positive','Only when the array is short','Mutation of a fresh accumulator can be deliberate; unintended shared-state mutation is the concern.','Map.set is a valid operation.','Numeric sign does not define mutation rules.','Length does not define the contract.'],
['A missing depot total should start at zero while an existing zero is valid. Which default fits?','acc.get(depot) ?? 0','Always use a random number','Always delete the depot','Always convert the key to true','Nullish fallback can supply zero for absence without discarding a meaningful zero value.','Random defaults corrupt totals.','Deletion loses the group.','Boolean conversion loses key identity.'],
['The grouped fixture returns North=5. What inputs produced it?','Counts two and three for North','All three counts regardless of key','The depot name’s length','The empty reduction’s initial zero only','Only rows with the North key contribute to that group’s total.','South belongs to a different group.','Names are not the count operands.','The separate empty reduction is independent.']
]},
{id:'js-modern-access',comp:'m4.c1',title:'Use template literals, spread and optional chaining deliberately',introduction:'A customer summary combines a name, a selected plan and optional contact details. Modern syntax can make that code clear, but it should not hide the difference between formatting a string, copying an outer object and validating external data.',sections:[s('Read each syntax feature’s role','Template literals use backticks and ${expression} interpolation. They build strings; they do not automatically escape HTML, SQL or shell syntax. Use context-appropriate safe APIs when the resulting value enters an interpreter. An interpolated string should not become untrusted innerHTML merely because the syntax is convenient.','Spread syntax expands iterable values in array/call contexts and copies own enumerable properties in object literals. The rightmost property can override an earlier one, so order matters. Rest syntax looks similar but collects remaining values in a parameter or destructuring context rather than expanding them.'),s('Access optional data without concealing errors','Optional chaining can simplify a known nullable field, and nullish coalescing can choose a fallback without replacing zero/false. These features do not validate a response schema or justify swallowing a missing required field. Give required-data failures useful error handling instead of filling every field with arbitrary defaults.','The worked example formats a string, applies an intentional property override and accesses an absent optional contact. It runs actual JavaScript with no DOM. Safe rendering, localization and server privacy rules remain separate responsibilities.')],worked:w('const base = {plan: "basic", active: true};\nconst selected = {...base, plan: "team"};\nconst customer = {name: "Demo", contact: null};\nconsole.log(`${customer.name}: ${selected.plan}`);\nconsole.log(customer.contact?.email ?? "No email supplied");\nconsole.log(base.plan);','Demo: team\nNo email supplied\nbasic','Format a summary and apply a shallow override without replacing the source plan.',['Expand the base properties into a new object.','Apply the later plan override.','Format supplied values and handle the optional contact explicitly.'],'The example creates strings and objects only. It does not prove safe HTML insertion or validate a remote customer record.'),mistakes:['Assuming interpolation automatically sanitizes every output context.','Using optional fallbacks to hide missing required data.'],takeaways:['Read syntax according to its specific role.','Keep rendering safety and schema validation explicit.'],quiz:[
['What does a template literal normally produce?','A string','A sanitized DOM tree','A database transaction','A CSS cascade layer','Template interpolation creates string content, not a universal safe representation for other interpreters.','DOM safety depends on insertion APIs and context.','No transaction is implicit.','It does not define CSS layers.'],
['In {...base, plan:"team"}, which plan wins?','The later team property','Always base.plan','Both values form an array','The longest string','Later object-literal properties override earlier properties with the same key.','Earlier spread values can be overridden.','No array is created automatically.','String length does not choose precedence.'],
['What does rest syntax do in parameters?','Collect remaining arguments','Expand a server database','Encrypt all values','Create a CSS selector','Rest parameters collect remaining arguments into an array.','It is a language binding operation.','No encryption occurs.','CSS is unrelated.'],
['A template includes a user-supplied name and is assigned to innerHTML. What is missing?','Context-appropriate safe rendering','Only a longer template variable','Only double quotes everywhere','Only object spread','Interpolation does not neutralize markup interpretation in an unsafe sink.','Names do not sanitize content.','Quote style alone is insufficient.','Copy syntax is unrelated to output encoding.'],
['A required response field is absent but ?? fills a fake success value. What is the issue?','The fallback hides a contract failure','Nullish coalescing validates all schemas','Optional syntax repairs the server','The fake value is authoritative','Required-data failures should not be silently converted into invented success.','The operator is not a schema validator.','Client access syntax does not repair server behavior.','Invented data is not evidence.'],
['An optional contact is null. What can contact?.email ?? "none" do?','Return the chosen fallback','Create a verified mailbox','Grant contact access','Deep-clone the customer','The chain yields undefined for the null contact and nullish coalescing supplies the string.','No mailbox verification occurs.','No permission is granted.','No clone is performed.'],
['A spread override creates a new top-level object. What should still be remembered?','Nested references can be shared','Every field is now immutable','Every source object was destroyed','All functions become strings','Object spread is shallow and does not freeze or recursively copy nested data.','No immutability is automatic.','The source can remain intact.','Functions are not universally serialized.'],
['The example leaves base.plan as basic. What does that show?','The later top-level override was separate','The entire object graph was deep-copied','The server saved a plan change','Every consumer sees team','The new object’s own plan property differs without changing that source property.','Nested graphs were not tested as independent.','No server operation occurs.','Only selected.plan is team here.']
]},
{id:'js-errors-contracts',comp:'m4.c2',title:'Handle errors with useful boundaries and custom types',introduction:'A ticket import can fail because a quantity is invalid or because an unexpected programming error occurred. Catching everything and pretending success loses evidence; useful error handling distinguishes expected domain failures from defects that should remain visible to developers.',sections:[s('Throw and catch at a meaningful boundary','Throw transfers control out of the current execution path until a matching catch handles it or it remains unhandled. Prefer Error objects or suitable subclasses so names, messages and stacks carry useful diagnostic context. A custom error can identify a domain category, but its message must not expose passwords, session values or private payloads.','Try/catch handles synchronous throws in the protected execution and awaited promise rejections when used appropriately in async code. It does not automatically catch an unrelated future timer callback just because the timer was scheduled inside the try block. Finally runs for cleanup under normal control-flow rules, but a return/throw inside finally can override earlier results and should be used cautiously.'),s('Recover only when the contract permits it','A catch can show a safe user-facing message for a known validation error while recording appropriate private diagnostics. Unknown errors should not be silently translated into a successful import count. Cleanup belongs in a deliberate finally or resource-management mechanism, with its own failure policy.','The worked example uses a custom synchronous error and records cleanup. It does not contact a remote system or demonstrate every asynchronous error path. The external application still needs request validation, authorization and safe monitoring.')],worked:w('class QuantityError extends Error { constructor(message) { super(message); this.name = "QuantityError"; } }\nfunction validateQuantity(value) { if (!Number.isInteger(value) || value <= 0) throw new QuantityError("Enter a positive integer."); return value; }\ntry { validateQuantity(0); } catch (error) { console.log(error.name + ": " + error.message); } finally { console.log("cleanup checked"); }','QuantityError: Enter a positive integer.\ncleanup checked','Categorize an invalid quantity and observe the finally block.',['Validate the supplied synthetic quantity.','Catch the explicit domain error and print its safe category/message.','Observe the cleanup marker separately from success.'],'The fixture covers this synchronous branch only. A cleanup marker is not evidence that a business operation succeeded.'),mistakes:['Catching every exception and returning invented success.','Returning from finally without considering overridden outcomes.'],takeaways:['Use meaningful error categories and safe messages.','Handle recovery, diagnostics and cleanup as separate responsibilities.'],quiz:[
['What does throw do to normal control flow?','Transfers control','Always returns zero','Only prints a warning','Always restarts the process','A throw exits the current path until handled or left unhandled.','No numeric return is automatic.','Throw is more than logging.','Restart depends on the surrounding system.'],
['Why use an Error subclass for a domain category?','Useful structured classification','Guaranteed secret encryption','Automatic server authorization','Automatic successful recovery','A type/name can let handlers distinguish known failures while retaining error diagnostics.','Errors do not encrypt messages.','Types are not permission checks.','Recovery requires actual policy.'],
['What is a typical use of finally?','Deliberate cleanup','Declaring every result successful','Hiding every exception','Creating every database row','Finally provides a cleanup/control-flow stage independent of the selected try/catch branch.','Cleanup is not success evidence.','It should not erase failures indiscriminately.','Resource behavior is not automatic data creation.'],
['A catch returns imported=100 after an unknown exception. What is the reliability problem?','It invents success evidence','It validates every row','It proves cleanup happened','It makes the server authorized','Unknown failure must not be misreported as a completed import.','No validation proof follows.','Cleanup is separate.','Authorization is unrelated to the invented result.'],
['A safe validation message includes the raw session token. What should change?','Remove credentials','Only rename the error class','Only add more stack frames','Only print the token twice','Error messages and diagnostics need disclosure controls, not credential leakage.','Names do not remove secret values.','More traces may expand exposure.','Repetition increases disclosure.'],
['A timer callback throws later after the surrounding try completed. What should not be assumed?','That earlier catch handles it','The callback is asynchronous','Its error needs an appropriate boundary','The timing differs from synchronous code','A catch around scheduling does not automatically cover a later callback invocation.','That is a relevant distinction.','An actual handler is needed.','Timing matters to the boundary.'],
['A finally block returns a new value. What must a reviewer consider?','It can override an earlier result','It always preserves the try return','It cannot affect control flow','It only changes CSS','Finally control flow can replace an earlier return or throw.','Preservation is not guaranteed.','It can alter the outcome.','CSS is unrelated.'],
['The fixture prints cleanup checked. What must not be inferred?','The quantity was accepted','The finally branch ran','The output was observed','The error category was available','The validation rejected zero; cleanup does not convert it into an accepted quantity.','That marker shows the branch execution.','The print is recorded.','The catch receives the error.']
]},
{id:'js-module-boundaries',comp:'m4.c1',title:'Organize modules and explicit public interfaces',introduction:'A dashboard team shares a pricing function between two features. Modules make dependencies and exported interfaces explicit, reducing accidental dependence on globals while preserving clear ownership of each function.',sections:[s('Imports are interfaces, not copied source files','ECMAScript modules use export and import declarations to expose and consume bindings. Named imports must match exported names; a default export has different import syntax. Module bindings are live views of exports rather than arbitrary copied text. Module scope differs from a classic browser script, and modules execute in strict mode.','Relative module specifiers depend on the execution environment’s resolution rules. A browser usually resolves URL-like module references, while Node and bundlers have their own package and file conventions. Do not assume a package name available in one build system is automatically understood by a browser without mapping or bundling.'),s('Keep side effects and deployment assumptions visible','A module can have initialization side effects even if a consumer imports only one function. Circular dependencies require care with initialization timing; splitting files does not automatically remove architectural coupling. Keep private server credentials out of browser-delivered modules because users can inspect delivered code.','The fixture dynamically imports a self-contained data-URL module inside Node, proving an actual export/import interaction without network access or package installation. It does not test browser URL loading, CORS, bundling or tree shaking. Those require separate environment-specific checks.')],worked:w('(async () => {\n  const source = "export const taxRate = 0.1; export function total(base) { return base * (1 + taxRate); }";\n  const moduleURL = "data:text/javascript," + encodeURIComponent(source);\n  const pricing = await import(moduleURL);\n  console.log(pricing.taxRate);\n  console.log(pricing.total(100).toFixed(2));\n})().catch(error => { console.error(error.message); process.exitCode = 1; });','0.1\n110.00','Import a self-contained real ECMAScript module without contacting a server.',['Build the declared module source as a data URL.','Await its dynamic import.','Read a named export and call an exported function.'],'The example verifies Node’s supported data-URL module path only. Browser loading, package resolution and financial rounding policy remain separate.'),mistakes:['Assuming every environment resolves module specifiers identically.','Putting server-only credentials into a browser module.'],takeaways:['Expose explicit interfaces and understand the runtime’s resolver.','A module boundary does not itself guarantee safe deployment or low coupling.'],quiz:[
['What does export make available?','A declared module interface','Every private server secret safely','Every browser global automatically','A new database schema','Exports define bindings that consumers can import through the module system.','Exporting secrets can disclose them.','Module exports are not all globals.','No schema operation is implied.'],
['What must a named import match?','An exported binding name','Only the file’s byte size','Only the branch name','Only the browser title','Named import syntax refers to the module’s declared exported names.','Size does not define names.','Git branches do not choose binding names.','Titles are unrelated.'],
['Which mode do ECMAScript modules use?','Strict mode','Always sloppy mode','Only a CSS compatibility mode','Only database safe mode','ECMAScript modules use strict-mode semantics.','Sloppy-mode assumptions do not apply.','CSS modes are unrelated.','Database settings are separate.'],
['A package specifier works in a bundler but not a plain browser. What should be checked?','Environment resolution rules','Only the string’s length','Only the logo image','Only the account password','Different environments resolve package and URL-like specifiers through different mechanisms.','Length does not establish resolver support.','Images do not resolve imports.','Passwords do not define module resolution.'],
['A module performs a request during initialization. What should a consumer recognize?','Import can have side effects','Imports only copy function text','Only default exports can execute','Every import is pure','Module evaluation can execute top-level code with side effects.','It is not textual copy-only behavior.','Named-export modules can also evaluate code.','Purity is not guaranteed.'],
['Two modules import each other. What needs care?','Initialization/coupling','Only their directory colour','Only their title tags','Only their source line count','Circular dependencies can expose uninitialized bindings and architectural coupling.','Presentation does not define initialization.','HTML titles are unrelated.','Line count alone does not resolve cycles.'],
['A browser bundle exports an API secret. What is the concern?','Delivered code is inspectable','Export syntax encrypts the value','Modules are automatically private vaults','Only admins can download JavaScript','Client-delivered modules are not safe storage for server-only secrets.','Exports do not encrypt.','Module scope is not delivery confidentiality.','Clients receiving code can inspect it.'],
['The data-URL import returns total 110.00. What was not tested?','Browser network module loading','The named export value','The exported function call','Node’s local module evaluation','The fixture has no browser network request, CORS negotiation or bundler build.','The rate is directly read.','The function is executed.','The module is evaluated by Node.']
]}
];
